   "html_text": "Informally, we might reason as follows. Either (7) is true or (7) is not true. Thus, either (8) is true or (9) is true. Thus, (7) is true. Thus (8) is true and (9) is not true, and (7) is still true. Iterating the process once again, we get (8) is true, (9) is not true, and (7) is true. More formally, consider any initial hypothesis, ⟦M⟧ , about the truth values of (7), (8) and (9). Either ⟦M⟧ says that (7) is true or ⟦M⟧ says that (7) is not true. In either case, we can use the T-biconditional ",
